### Early Attestation
- **Presentation:** [Early Attestation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-seat-early-attestation-02)
- **Presenter:** Yaron Sheffer
- **Draft:** `draft-fossati-seat-early-attestation-09`

- **Presentation Summary:**
  - Yaron Sheffer detailed the intra-handshake mechanism where attestation parameters are negotiated during the TLS handshake via client/server extensions and certificate entries, bound securely to the TLS handshake transcript and TLS key.
  - Detailed the re-attestation design utilizing TLS Extended Key Update (EKU). Session resumption and 0-RTT early data are disabled when EKU re-attestation is used.
  - Described the passport model flow using wrapped attestation results (CMW) bound to the active TLS handshake.
  - Referenced an end-to-end proof-of-concept implementation developed by Arm.

- **Discussion:**
  - **Unauthenticated Privacy Concerns:** Kubilay Küçük and Muhammad Usama Sardar expressed concerns over leaking hardware identifiers to unauthenticated clients before client authentication completes. Yaron Sheffer explained that in TLS server authentication, providing server evidence to an unauthenticated client is structurally unavoidable within intra-handshake server authentication, and suggested claim minimization.
  - **Re-attestation & Lifecycle:** Shalini asked how EKU-based re-attestation applies during virtual machine migration events. Yaron Sheffer noted that platform events can trigger EKU, which in turn forces re-attestation.
  - **Adoption Support:** Yuxuan Song voiced support for adopting the draft as a solid baseline for intra-handshake attestation.

